# Untitled

In situ growth CNT@MOFs core–shell structures enabling high specific supercapacitances in neutral aqueous electrolyte
Mingxia Lu<sup>1</sup>, Gang Wang<sup>(</sup>, Xiping Yang<sup>1</sup>, and Bo Hou<sup>(</sup>,<sup>(</sup>)
<sup>1</sup> School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Henan University of Technology, Zhengzhou 450001, China  <sup>2</sup> School of Physics and Astronomy, Cardiff University, Cardiff CF24 3AA, UK
© Tsinghua University Press 2022
## ABSTRACT
Conductive metal-organic frameworks (c-MOFs) are promising active electrode materials for electrochemical double-layer capacitors with a performance that already exceeds most carbon-based materials. However, their excellent supercapacitance is primarily based on organic or alkaline electrolytes, which largely impede their broad applications and sustainabilities. In this work, we propose a new synthesis approach for fabricating carbon nanotubes and c-MOFs (CNT@MOFs) core–shell structures, which result in high supercapacitance in neutral aqueous electrolytes. We identify that CNTs provide abundant active sites to ensure high capacitance, and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11\)-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP))<sub>2</sub> nanoarrays that in situ grow on the surface of CNTs bundles can significantly improve the conductivity and provide enough ion transport pathways in aqueous electrolytes. Specifically, using CNT@MOFs core–shell structures as an electrode, we obtained a high initial capacitance of \(150.7\ \mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\ \mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution and good capacity retention of \(63.5\%\) after 10,000 cycles at \(4\ \mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\). We also found that the carboxyl groups on the surface of CNTs provide better anchor sites for the in situ growth of c-MOF, which promotes the uniform growth of c-MOF shells on the CNT surface and improves aqueous electrolyte accessibility. We believe that the high supercapacitance in aqueous electrolytes reported in this work would provide a good prospect for deploying c-MOF based energy storage devices into biomedical and other healthcare electronic applications.
## KEYWORDS
carbon nanotubes, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), conductive MOFs, supercapacitor, neutral aqueous electrolyte
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), due to their tunable structure, rich micropore structure, and high specific surface area [1–3] have been widely researched and applied in many fields, such as supercapacitors [4, 5], batteries [6, 7], electrocatalysis [8, 9], sensors [10], drug delivery [11, 12], and recently reported airborne water harvest [13–15]. However, the poor electrical conductivity and stability of most traditional MOFs, in particular, their poor electrochemical performance in aqueous electrolytes, significantly limits their applications [16–18]. Generally, there are two strategies to address this challenge: i) developing new MOFs materials with high conductivity and stable structure [19], ii) compositing with high conductive materials, such as graphene, carbon nanotubes (CNTs), and conductive polymers [20, 21].
So far, the most challenging and potentially rewarding approach is to synthesis new MOFs materials with high charge mobility and electrical conductivity. Since the discovery of \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\) (conductivity: \(1\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S\cdot m^{-1}}\)) in 2009 by Takaishi [22], nearly 20 types of conductive MOFs (c-MOFs) were designed and synthesised, such as \(\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{3})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Mg}, \mathrm{Mn}, \mathrm{Fe}, \mathrm{Co}, \mathrm{Cu}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{Zn})\) [23], \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) [24], \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{DXBDC})\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Mg}, \mathrm{Fe}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{X}=\mathrm{O}, \mathrm{S})\) [25, 26], \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{TTFB})\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Mn}, \mathrm{Co}, \mathrm{Zn}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{Cd})\) [27], \(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HXTP})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}, \mathrm{Ni}, \mathrm{Cu}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{Pt}; \mathrm{X}=\mathrm{O}, \mathrm{S}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{NH})\) [4, 28–31], M-HAB \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}, \mathrm{Ni}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{Cu})\) [32], and \(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTT})_{2}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Ni}, \mathrm{and} \mathrm{Cu})\) [33]. Among them, two
dimensional (2D) c-MOFs have shown the highest conductivity values, most likely due to the in-plane charge delocalization and extended \(\pi\)-conjugation in the 2D sheets [34]. Sheberla and coworkers have reported a 2D c-MOF \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11\)-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP))<sub>2</sub>, which has a bulk (pellet) conductivity value of \(200\ \mathrm{S\cdot m^{-1}}\) [35]. As the electrode of the supercapacitor, \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) exhibited a capacitance of \(102\ \mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1\ \mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) and remained \(90\%\) after 10,000 cycles in \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{CEABF}_{4}/\mathrm{CAN}\) electrolyte. \(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HXTP})_{2}\), \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{HHTT})_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{M}\)-HAB are \(\pi\)-stacked 2D c-MOFs with an extended \(\pi\)-conjugate plane structure, similar to graphite. The high conductivity of 2D c-MOFs originates from their extended \(\pi\)-conjugate structure, giving it more \(\pi\)-\(\pi\) and \(\pi\)-d orbital overlap, while \(\pi\)-\(\pi\)-stacking between layers also enables spatial charge transfer [36, 37]. However, further improving the capacitance and energy density is challenging. Currently, adding electrochemically active materials as additives and composite materials into c-MOF is well acknowledged as an efficient approach. For example, assembling MOFs with high conductive carbon materials, such as graphene, CNTs, carbon black, and so on, has become an effective strategy for improving energy storage efficiency [21, 38, 39].
Among these materials, CNTs exhibit high electrical conductivity, large surface area, and excellent cycle life, which enable them to be the most promising conductive composite materials [40]. For example, Wen and co-workers synthesized Ni
MOF/CNT composites, which exhibited good electrochemical activity due to the synergism between the specific structure of pristine Ni-MOF and the high conductivity of CNTs, achieving a high specific capacitance of \(1,765\;\;\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(0.5\;\;\mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) [21]. CuMOF/CNT hybrids synthesized by Ansari and co-workers for supercapacitor applications revealed a high specific capacitance of \(380\;\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(1.6\;\mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) with a good rate performance [41].
More importantly, these results suggest that the introduction of conductive CNTs into porous MOFs structures enhanced not only the mechanical strength and conductivity but also the electrolyte accessibility throughout the charge–discharge process [18, 42, 43]. For instance, it was reported that the high conductivity of CNTs can improve the electron transport from the active material to the collector [21, 41]. Therefore, introducing conductive CNTs into porous MOFs structures is a feasible strategy for exploring the possibility of MOFs supercapacitance electrochemical energy storage in neutral aqueous electrolytes. Neutral aqueous electrolytes have the advantages of safety, simple manufacturing, low cost, and environmental friendliness. More importantly, the ionic conductivity of aqueous electrolytes is usually much higher than that of organic electrolytes, which leads to a better rate performance.
Herein, a \(\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{MOF}\) core–shell structure was synthesized by the "bottom-up" approach which directly grew \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) (cMOF) on the CNTs' surface. A benchmark symmetric supercapacitor (SC) configuration was employed for evaluating the electrochemical supercapacitive performance of \(\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{MOF}\) in neutral aqueous electrolytes. Furthermore, different amounts of CNTs were tested to explore the effect of CNTs loading on the final morphological of \(\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{MOF}\) composites and their electrochemical energy storage performance. We found that the carboxyl groups on the surface of CNTs provided better anchor sites for the in situ growth of c-MOF, which promoted the uniform growth of c-MOF shells on the CNTs surface and improved aqueous electrolyte accessibility. However, the MOF/CNT-0.5 with the CNTs content of \(13.09\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) showed the best specific capacitance, rate performance, and cyclic durability.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
Nickel chloride hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) and aqueous ammonium \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) were purchased from Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Technologies Co., Ltd (China). 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenylene hexahydrochloride (HATP-6HCl) was purchased from Zhengzhou Jifa Chemical Co., Ltd (China). The above chemicals were used directly without further purification. Single-walled CNTs (SWCNTs, purity \(>95\%\), external diameter \(<2\;\mathrm{nm}\), and length \(0.3{-}5\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\)) purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation were used after oxidation treatment by the mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) (98%) and \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) (60%) (3:1 v/v). Specific methods can refer to our previously published works [38, 44].
## 2.2 Synthesis of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) powder
\(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was synthesized by mixing HATP-6HCl to \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) following the procedure reported by Sheberla and coworkers [35]. Typically, the \(40\;\mathrm{mg}\) of HATP-6HCl was dissolved in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized (DI) water under continuous ultrasonication for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and then transferred into a \(100\;\mathrm{mL}\) flask. Meanwhile, the \(26.4\;\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was dissolved in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) of DI water, added \(1.2\;\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (28%), and transferred to a \(25\;\mathrm{mL}\) constant pressure drop funnel. After that, the \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution was dropwise added into the flask under stirring and the dropping speed was 1 drop every 2 s under control. This mixture was stirred in a flask for \(2\;\mathrm{h}\) at \(65\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The resulting black powder was filtered by a microporous membrane \((0.22\;\upmu\mathrm{m})\), washed with a large amount of water and acetone \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COCH}_{3}\right)\), and dried at \(80\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight. The final collected black powder was named c-MOF.
