## 2.3 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{CNTs}@\mathbf{Ni}_{3}(\mathbf{HITP})_{2}\) powder
The preparation of \(\mathrm{CNTs}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) composites followed the same procedures as that of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) powder, except that the HATP-6HCl \((40\;\mathrm{mg})\) was dissolved in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) of CNTs aqueous solution instead of DI water. Typically, a series of CNTs dispersions with a concentration of \(0.2\), \(0.5\), and \(1.0\;\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\) were prepared by dispersing CNTs powder into DI water under continuous ultrasonication (KQ-300VDE, \(45\;\mathrm{kHz}\), \(8\;\mathrm{h}\)). After that, HATP-6HCl \((40\;\mathrm{mg})\) was dissolved in CNTs aqueous solution with further \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) ultrasonication. Then, the preparation of \(\mathrm{CNTs}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) hybrids followed the same procedures as that of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) powder. For the different amounts of CNTs loading, \(\mathrm{CNTs}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) hybrids were denoted as c-MOF/CNT-0.2, c-MOF/CNT-0.5, and c-MOF/CNT-1.0, with the CNTs concentration of \(0.2\), \(0.5\), and \(1.0\;\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\), respectively.
## 2.4 Characterization
The morphology was carried out by field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Zeiss Sigma 300, Germany) with \(10\;\mathrm{kV}\) operating voltage. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images were acquired using Titan G260-300 microscopy (FEI, USA). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was employed to identify the functional groups on the materials by a Nicolet Avatar 360 FTIR spectrometer (FTIR, Instrument Co., Madison, WI, USA). Raman spectra were collected on a LabRAM HR Evolution spectroscopy with an excitation wavelength of \(532\;\mathrm{nm}\) (HOLIBA Jobin Yvon, France). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded using a Rigaku MiniFlex 600 X-ray diffractometer (Rigaku Co., Tokyo, Japan) with \(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation over the \(2\theta\) range of \(3^{\circ}{-}80^{\circ}\). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha instrument (Thermo Scientific Inc., USA). The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms were measured by a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 gas sorption analyzer (Micromeritics Instrument Corp., USA) at \(77\;\mathrm{K}\). The electrical conductivity was performed on an RTS-9 (4 PROBES TECH, Guangzhou, China) four-point probes station.
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurements
