The gravimetric specific capacitance (Cs) was calculated from the GCD discharge curves using Eqs. (1) and (2)
\[
C_s(\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}})=\int I(U)\mathrm{d}U/(2\cdot m\cdot v\cdot\Delta U)
\]
\[
C_s(\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}})=I\cdot\Delta t/(m\cdot\Delta U)
\]
where \(I\) is the discharging current (A), \(\Delta t\) is the discharging time (s), \(m\) is the mass of active material on one electrode (g), and \(\Delta U\) (V) is the potential discharging range.
The SC was fabricated with \(1.0\mathrm{~M~Na_{2}S O_{4}}\) aqueous solution to evaluate the energy density. c-MOF/CNT-0.5 and c-MOF were immersed in \(1.0\mathrm{~M~Na_{2}S O_{4}}\) aqueous solution for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) and then separated by filter paper soaked with electrolyte. The GCD test was performed at a current density of \(1\,\mathrm{\A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) with a potential range of \(-0.2–0.4\,\mathrm{V}\).
Cs was calculated based on the total mass of two electrodes
\[
C_s(\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}})=4I\cdot\Delta t/(m\cdot\Delta U)
\]
\[
C_s(\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}})=\int I(U)\mathrm{d}U/(2\cdot m\cdot v\cdot\Delta U)
\]
where \(\upsilon\) is the scan rate \((\mathrm{V}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1})\).
## 3 Results and discussion
The fabrication processes of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) and c-MOF/CNT composites are schematically shown in Figs. 1(a) and 1(b). \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) powder was prepared via the chemical reaction between HATP·6HCl and \(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in \(\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}\) at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After being filtered, rinsed, and dried, the black powder of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was obtained. It features a 2D hexagonal lattice structure by coordinating Ni ions and HATP ligands in a long-range order with the AB stacking arrangement within the \(a b\) plane. Compared with traditional MOFs, the high conductivity of c-MOF originates from its own extended \(\pi\) -conjugate structure, which offers a more \(\pi{-}\pi\) and \(\pi{-}\mathrm{d}\) orbital overlap. In addition, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking between layers also enables spatial charge transfer. The effective orbital
The morphological structures of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) and c-MOF/CNT were characterized at different scales via scanning electron microscopy (SEM) analysis. Figures 2(a) and 2(b) show the structure and morphology of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\). The finger-like structure is composed of a stacking layered \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) nanosheet with a width of about \(10{-}20\;\mathrm{nm}\) and a length of \(100{-}200\;\mathrm{nm}\). The fingerlike structure is self-assembled again to form nanoarrays, and then scrolled to form a ring structure (Fig. 2(c) shows the model of structure). This structure was further demonstrated in the following transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis. As shown in Fig. S2(b) in the ESM, the internal diameter of the "ring" is about \(50{-}100\,\mathrm{~nm}\) and the external diameter is about \(100-\) \(200~\mathrm{~nm}\). The structure of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) is a 2D in-plane \(\pi\) - conjugated material. In \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\), Ni atom adopts \(\mathrm{dsp}^{2}\) hybridization, forming a 2D conjugation. Their morphology was further confirmed by TEM (Fig. 3 and Fig. S3 in the ESM).
As shown in Figs. 2(d) and 2(e), the SEM picture of c-MOF/CNT-0.5 shows a large of uniformly growing nanoarrays on the nanotube bundles, indicating that \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) has been successfully assembled on the CNTs surface. However, the presence of particles is rarely observed on the thinner CNTs bundles, suggesting the difficulty of composites on the nanotubes with a smaller radius of curvature. The structure model of the c-MOF/CNT composite is shown in Fig. 2(f). Meanwhile, the c-MOF can be evenly loaded onto the CNTs bundles. When CNTs are overloaded, few nanoarrays can be observed.
The SEM images of c-MOF/CNT composites with different CNTs contents are shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Because of the
difficulties of dispersing CNTs powder into DI water, CNTs usually tend to be stacked together to form bundles. In Fig. S4 in the ESM, we can observe that the diameter of the bundle increases with the increasing CNTs content.
Based on the SEM analysis, we propose that the c-MOF molecules are coupled via weak metal–metal interaction and strongly bonded on the CNTs surface by the way of \(\pi{-}\pi\) conjugation. To verify our hypothesis, the detailed morphological features of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) and c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite were investigated by HRTEM measurement. The morphological structure of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) shown in Figs. 3(a) and 3(b) is consistent with the SEM results. As shown in Figs. 3(c) and 3(d), \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) presents clear hexagonal lattices of \(1.90\;\mathrm{nm}\) indicating parallel one-dimensional (1D) pores, consistent with the periodic arrangement of hexaiminotriphenylenes linked by the square planar metal centers. The crystalline nature of CNTs bundles and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) can be easily recognized from the HRTEM images of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite (Figs. 3(e) and 3(f)). HRTEM image of c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite (Fig. 3(g)) shows that the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) bonded on the CNTs surface also presents clear hexagonal lattices of \(1.97\ \mathrm{nm}\) . The \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking distance of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite is measured as \(0.360\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3(h)), which is consistent with the \(d\) -spacing between the crystalline (001) plane of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) [8]. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. 3(i)) showing diffused ring patterns indicates poor crystallinity. Selected area TEM elemental mapping (Fig. 3(j)) was employed to further confirm the uniform distribution of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite. As depicted in Figs. \(3(\mathrm{k}){-}3(\mathrm{n})\) , the characteristic elements for c-MOF/CNT-0.5, including C, N, O, and Ni, are evenly distributed on the surface of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5.
FTIR analysis was carried out to investigate the chemical structure of c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT powder with different CNTs contents. As shown in Fig. 4(a), the absorption peak at \(3,430~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belongs to \(\mathrm{N-H}\) stretching vibration on \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{O-H}\) hydroxyl stretching vibration of adsorbed water. The peak located at \(1,620~\ c m^{-1}\) was caused by \(C{=}C\) benzene skeleton vibration. The signals observed at 1,520 and \(1,340\ \ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) represented the antisymmetric and symmetrical stretching vibrations of \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2},\) which indicated that the partial \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) groups were oxidized. The other peaks, such as 1,440, 1,360–1,020, and \(900{-}650~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) , corresponded to \(\mathrm{C-H}\) in-plane bending vibration, \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}\) stretching vibration, and \(\mathrm{C-H}\) or \(\mathrm{N-H}\) out-of-plane bending vibration, respectively. The presence of \(\mathrm{N-H},\) \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C},\) and \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}\)
demonstrated the successful synthesis of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) . The similar absorption spectra of c-MOF and \(\mathrm{CNT}@c\) -MOF with different CNTs loading ratios suggested that c-MOF was successfully assembled on the surface of CNTs rather than a simple mixing process.
In addition, Raman spectroscopy was employed to study the structural changes of c-MOF/CNT composite as a function of CNTs contents. As shown in Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S5 in the ESM, two peaks centered at 1,580 and \(1,320~\ c m^{-1}\) (G-band and D-band respectively) can be revealed, which are usually associated with the vibration of \(\mathbf{sp}^{2}\) hybrid C atoms and the defect or disorder structure, respectively. The peak intensity ratio of the D-band and G-band \((I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})\) of c-MOF is about 0.987, which is considerably higher than c-MOF/CNT-0.2 (0.075), c-MOF/CNT-0.5 (0.153), and c-MOF/CNT-1.0 (0.075), indicating the high disorder degree in \(\mathbf{sp}^{2}\) C (Fig. 4(c)). Compared with other c-MOF/CNT composites, c-MOF/CNT-0.5 shows the highest \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) value, implying more defects or disorders can enable more active sites for energy storage.
To better understand the crystal structure changes of c-MOF/CNT composites as a function of CNTs contents, the XRD analysis of c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT composites was performed and shown in Fig. 4(d). The prominent peaks at \(2\theta=4.7^{\circ}\) , \(9.5^{\circ}\) , \(12.6^{\circ}\) , \(16.5^{\circ}\) , and \(27.3^{\circ}\) , indicate the successful synthesis of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) crystals. The diffraction peak at \(2\theta=4.7^{\circ}\) is attributed to the (100) crystal plane of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) with an interlayer spacing of \(2.09\,\mathrm{~nm}\) . The weaker and broader peak at \(2\theta\:=\:27.3^{\circ}\) is attributed to the (001) crystal plane with an interlayer spacing of \(0.36\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , which is consistent with the TEM results. Compared with \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},\) no significant changes are observed for the peak position and full width half maximum (FWHM) of these peaks in c-MOF/CNT composites, indicating that \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) was successfully assembled on the surface of CNTs. However, as the CNTs content increases, a slight displacement is observed (Fig. 4(e) and Fig. S6 in the ESM), which indicates that the addition of CNTs might alter the crystal structure of c-MOF via additional \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking and interaction.
\(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and pore size distributions of c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT-0.5 are presented in Fig. 4(f) and Fig. S7 in the ESM. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas of c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT-0.5 were determined to be 589.09 and \(753.22\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively (Table S1 in the ESM). The higher specific surface area of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 indicates a higher micropore volume (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The results were
further confirmed with pore size distribution curves based on nonlocal density functional theory (DFT) calculation which is a typical method for extracting pore size distributions from nanoporous materials (Fig. S7 in the ESM) [1]. The curves show peaks centered at around \(0.95\,\mathrm{nm}\), which suggests the existence of microporous structure in c-MOF. Furthermore, the intensity of the centered differential pore volume peak of c-MOF/CNT-0.5 is higher than that of c-MOF, exhibiting an improvement of the microporous structure.
XPS measurements were carried out to investigate the chemical composition and elemental oxidation state. From the wide-scanned XPS spectra shown in Fig. 5(a), the existence of Ni 2p, O 1s, N 1s, and C 1s resonance peaks can be observed. The contents
of C, N, O, and Ni elements are \(68.14\%\), \(12.64\%\), \(17.85\%\), and \(1.37\%\) for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\), and \(70.42\%\), \(11.49\%\), \(16.95\%\), and \(1.13\%\) for c-MOF/CNT-0.5. The high O content is due to the partial oxidation of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) during the synthesis process. The presence of CNTs reduced the content of O in the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 composite. The C 1s spectrum were divided into three main peaks centered at 284.3, 285.6, and \(287.7\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\), and 284.3, 285.1, and \(287.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) for c-MOF/CNT-0.5. These peaks are attributed to \(\mathrm{C=C/C-C}\), C–N, and \(\mathrm{C=O}\) bonds, respectively (Fig. 5(c)). As shown in Fig. 5(d), the N 1s spectrum is mainly divided into three peaks with the binding energies of 397.4, 399.1, and 401.7 eV corresponding to N–Ni, graphitic–N, and N–H, respectively. The XPS spectra of O 1s fitted with two prominent
peaks are assigned to \(\mathrm{O}{=}\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{C-OH}\) with the binding energies of 531.1 and \(282.3~\mathrm{eV},\) respectively (Fig. 5(e)). Specifically, the Ni 2p spectra are fitted with four individual peaks, which are belonging to Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2},\) Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) satellite, Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2},\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) satellite with the binding energies of 855.4, 861.7, 873.0, and \(879.8\;\;\mathrm{eV},\) respectively (Fig.5(f)), confirming that the oxidation state of Ni in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) is \(+2\) . Compared with pristine \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},\) it is clear that the binding energies of Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) in \(\mathrm{c-MOF/CNT-0.5}\) are almost the same as \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) . It indicates that there are no changes in the chemical composition or chemical environment of Ni in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) after composing.
To investigate the electrochemical properties of the prepared electrodes, CV, GCD, and EIS measurements were performed under a three-electrode cell configuration in a neutral aqueous electrolyte \((1.0\mathrm{~M~Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})\) . As shown in Fig. 6(a), the electrodes based on c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT composites show characteristic rectangular CV curves without significant redox peaks, indicating typical double-layer capacitance properties. In addition, the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode exhibits a larger integral area than c-MOF and other c-MOF/CNT composites electrodes, which indicates a higher specific capacity and more electrochemical active sites. Figure 6(b) shows the CV curves of c-MOF/CNT-0.5 at various scan rates between 5 and \(100\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) in a working potential window of \(-0.2–0.4\mathrm{~\,~V~}\) . The specific capacitances of all electrodes at different scan rates calculated by Eq. (1) are shown in Fig. 6(c). Thus, the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode has a higher specific capacitance than other electrodes. Also, GCD curves suggest the same situation.
As shown in Fig. 6(d), the GCD curves of c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT electrodes are symmetrical and linear, indicating excellent electrochemical reversibility. The GCD curves of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode at various current densities are shown in Fig. 6(e), which are linear and highly symmetric, and the infrared (IR) drop is low indicating the excellent stability in the charge–discharge process. As depicted in Fig. S8(b) in the ESM, the IR drop is lower at a small current density. It increases gradually with increasing current densities. Furthermore, the IR drop of the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode is much lower than that of the other c-MOF/CNT electrode and pristine c-MOF electrodes at the same current density. This means that the overall resistance reduces significantly. The specific capacitances of all electrodes calculated from the GCD curves by Eq. (2) are shown in Fig. 6(f). As the current density increases from 0.1 to \(5\;\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , c-MOF/CNT
0.5 and c-MOF maintain \(35\%\) (from 150.7 to \(53.3\;\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ) and \(14\%\) (from 122.8 to \(17.5\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) ) of their initial capacitances, indicating that c-MOF/CNT-0.5 has a better rate capability than c-MOF. Furthermore, rate capability increases with increasing CNTs content, which can be confirmed by the slop of the curves in Fig. S9 in the ESM. The smaller absolute value of the slope indicates the better rate capability.
EIS measurement provided helpful information to explain this phenomenon. The Nyquist plots (Fig 6(g) and Fig. S10 in the ESM) of all electrodes include two regions, an incomplete semicircle obtained in high-frequency areas and a linear tail received in low frequency, respectively. Compared with the other electrodes, c-MOF/CNT-0.5 shows a much smaller charge transfer resistance \((R_{\mathrm{ct}})\) , and a more vertical tail means a faster charge transfer and excellent capacitive behavior. To better understand the frequency response of the electrode, the relaxation time constant \(t_{0}\) was calculated, as shown in Fig. 6(h). Typically, \(t_{0}\) represents the minimum time required to discharge the stored energy with \(50\%\) efficiency [46]. All c-MOF/CNT electrodes have similar \(t_{0},\) which is slightly smaller than the value of c-MOF, indicating the faster charge–discharge property characteristics and the most facile penetration of ions into pores. Cyclic stability results for c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT-0.5 are provided in Fig. 6(i). Both c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT-0.5 exhibit good cycle stability, retaining above \(82\%\) after 10,000 charge–discharge cycles at a current density of \(4~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . In conclusion, the addition of CNTs effectively improves the electrochemical properties of c-MOF materials.
To explore the practical application value of c-MOF, c-MOF/CNT-0.5, the symmetric capacitor was assembled with c-MOF, c-MOF/CNT-0.5 as working electrodes in \(1.0\mathrm{~M~Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) (Fig. S11 in the ESM). The CV curves (Figs. 7(a) and 7(b)) display an ideal rectangle and a slight deviation with the increasing scan rates (5 to \(100\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) ). Similarly, the GCD curves (Fig. 7(c) and 7(d)) also exhibit a highly symmetrical triangle in \(0.1{-}2\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , reflecting excellent rate performance and fast reaction kinetics. The specific capacitances calculated by GCD discharging time as a function of the current densities are plotted in Fig. 7(e). The result shows that the specific capacitance of the device is almost one-fourth of that of a single electrode. We can see that the c-MOF/CNT-0.5//c-MOF/CNT-0.5 symmetric capacitor has a higher specific capacitance than the c-MOF//c-MOF symmetric
capacitor at current densities from 0.1 to \(3\;\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). As the current density increases from 0.1 to \(3\;\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), c-MOF/c-MOF and c-MOF/CNT-0.5/c-MOF/CNT-0.5 maintain \(14.5\%\) (from 29.0 to \(4.2\;\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) and \(46.7\%\) (from 28.5 to \(13.3\;\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) of their initial capacitances, indicating that c-MOF/CNT-0.5/c-MOF/CNT-0.5 has a better rate capability than c-MOF/c-MOF.
The relaxation time constant \(t_{0}\) was plotted for a better comparison of the frequency responses of the electrodes (Fig. 7(f)). c-MOF/CNT-0.5/c-MOF/CNT-0.5 device exhibited a shorter \(t_{0}\) and a smaller \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) (inset of Fig. 7(f)) than those of c-MOF/c-MOF, revealing that the addition of CNTs had a positive effect on the
electro-adsorption/desorption kinetics. Meanwhile, only one peak is observed in Fig. 7(f), which indicates that there is only one primary charge storage mechanism, which is more likely due to ion adsorption.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we synthesized the composite CNT@c-MOF by directly growing \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) on the CNTs surface and demonstrated the potential application in high-performance supercapacitors in neutral aqueous electrolytes. We identified that
CNTs provide abundant active sites to ensure high capacitance, and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\) nanoarrays that in situ grow on the surface of CNTs bundles can significantly improve the conductivity and provide enough ion transport pathways. It is confirmed that the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode has higher specific capacitance and better rate capability than those of other c-MOF/CNT electrodes and c-MOF electrodes in both of a three-electrode system and aqueous device. In a three-electrode system, the c-MOF/CNT-0.5 electrode has a specific capacitance of \(150.7~\mathrm{F\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(0.1~\mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) in 1 M \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, maintaining \(83.5\%\) of the primitive capacitance after 10,000 cycles at \(4~\mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\). Our results demonstrate that CNT@c-MOF is a promising material platform for addressing the challenge of applying c-MOF into neutral aqueous electrolytes for high-performance electrochemical energy storage applications. We also envisaged that conductive CNT@c-MOF could be a new kind of promising microporous electrode material for other wearable energy storage and gas storage applications.
